# HTTP client: Axios only

When implementing outbound HTTP calls (Client browser requests, API proxies, webhooks, third-party integrations), **always use [Axios](https://axios-http.com/)**.

Do **not** use:

- `fetch` / `globalThis.fetch`
- `XMLHttpRequest` / `xhr`
- `node-fetch`, `got`, `undici`, `superagent`, or other HTTP clients

## Setup

Add Axios if the package is not already installed:

```bash
npm install axios
```

Use ESM import style consistent with the file:

```javascript
import axios from "axios";
```

## Examples

```javascript
// ❌ BAD — fetch
const response = await fetch(url);
const data = await response.json();

// ✅ GOOD — Axios
const { data } = await axios.get(url);
```

```javascript
// ❌ BAD — manual status checks with fetch
const response = await fetch(url);
if (!response.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${response.status}`);

// ✅ GOOD — Axios throws on 4xx/5xx; handle with try/catch
try {
  const { data } = await axios.get(url);
  res.json(data);
} catch (err) {
  const status = err.response?.status ?? 502;
  const message = err.response?.data ?? err.message;
  res.status(status).json({ error: message });
}
```

POST with JSON body:

```javascript
const { data } = await axios.post(url, { key: "value" });
```

## Refactoring existing code

If you touch a file that still uses `fetch` for HTTP, replace it with Axios in the same change.
